Package: stt-button
Version: 0.1.0
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: salmon <salmon@localhost>
Depends: python3, python3-venv, python3-pip, pipewire-bin, pulseaudio-utils, wtype
Recommends: wl-clipboard, dotool, gnome-shell
Description: Push-to-Talk dictation (Copilot key -> Parakeet ASR -> text)
 Local push-to-talk dictation: hold the Copilot key, speak, and the
 recognized text (local via Parakeet TDT 0.6B, ONNX/CPU) is streamed
 to the cursor position of the focused window.
 .
 Runtime design: the package contains the Python code, a
 self-healing launcher (/usr/bin/stt-button), the systemd user unit and
 a requirements profile. On the first invocation a per-user Python
 environment (venv under ~/.local/stt-button/venv) is set up with the
 ML stack (torch[cpu], transformers, onnxruntime, ...) (~5-15 min,
 one-time). The ASR model is loaded by the engine from Hugging Face
 automatically on the first dictation (~200 MB, gets cached).
 .
 After installation: 'stt-button setup' (venv + config + user unit),
 then set the key and audio source in
 ~/.config/stt-button/config.toml and 'systemctl --user restart
 stt-button'. 'dotool' is optional (the default typer is 'wtype',
 which is in the Ubuntu repos); 'gnome-shell' is only needed when the
 focused window shall be detected via a Shell extension.
